#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133566 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133757 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fba8401-2da1-50f2-8e96-84cbe06d95fc" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of the Dot Product"}] :question/id #uuid "c374456a-bff7-5145-a590-620bbf50dabf" :question/math-academy-id "e-5536" :question/problem "Find $(5\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}) \\cdot \\mathbf{c}+(4\\mathbf{b}+2\\mathbf{c}) \\cdot \\mathbf{d},$ if $\\mathbf{c} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = 2,$ $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{c} = -1,$ $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{d} = 2,$ and $\\mathbf{d} \\cdot \\mathbf{c}=3.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the fact that the dot product is linear in the first variable, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5a+3b)\\cdot c+(4b+2c)\\cdot d & =(5a)\\cdot c+(3b)\\cdot c+(4b)\\cdot d+(2c)\\cdot d \\\\  & =5(a\\cdot c)+3(b\\cdot c)+4(b\\cdot d)+2(c\\cdot d).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we can substitute the values for $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{c}$ and $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{d},$ and use the fact that the dot product is symmetric to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(a\\cdot c)+3(b\\cdot c)+4(b\\cdot d)+2(c\\cdot d) & =5(c\\cdot a)+3(-1)+4(2)+2(d\\cdot c) \\\\  & =5(c\\cdot a)-3+8+2(d\\cdot c) \\\\  & =5(c\\cdot a)+5+2(d\\cdot c).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the values for $\\mathbf{c} \\cdot \\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{d} \\cdot \\mathbf{c},$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(c\\cdot a)+5+2(d\\cdot c) & =5(2)+5+2(3) \\\\  & =10+5+6 \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(5\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}) \\cdot \\mathbf{c}+(4\\mathbf{b}+2\\mathbf{c}) \\cdot \\mathbf{d} = 21.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133567 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41c2d289-8df5-551b-9501-3a762c67386f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Dot Product"}] :question/id #uuid "13909d3e-b3df-5fcb-9056-5d71283fbebe" :question/math-academy-id "e-5537" :question/problem "Suppose $(4\\mathbf{a} + 2\\mathbf{b}) \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = {0},$ where $\\mathbf{a} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = -2(\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{a})$\n\n- $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} =  0$\n\n- $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = 1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4a+2b)\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ (4a)\\cdot a+(2b)\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ 4(a\\cdot a)+2(b\\cdot a) & =0 \\\\ 2(b\\cdot a) & =-4(a\\cdot a) \\\\ b\\cdot a & =-2(a\\cdot a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\mathbf{a} \\neq \\mathbf{0},$ we must have $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} > 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false since it leads to a contradiction. Simplifying $(4\\mathbf{a} + 2\\mathbf{b}) \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = {0}$ and substituting in $\\mathbf{b} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = 1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4a+2b)\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ 4a\\cdot a+2b\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ 4a\\cdot a+2(1) & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot a & =-\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbut this is a contradiction since $\\mathbf{a} \\neq \\mathbf{0}$ implies that $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} > 0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133628 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133813 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98eb5973-f9b1-5c2f-8763-e8f8707e9b89" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Dot Product"}] :question/id #uuid "7000f188-3223-5a2c-afe6-45fd3f92b8c6" :question/math-academy-id "e-5907" :question/problem "If ![](images/8d/8d9347b89864ea011c9dd3fbd17b94f05c479ff7a96343aae7928b241491c3ba.png) ![](images/b5/b5521921f5997e3cb748c518a9823574fcdfb82cd4bb1d1f7bbbf17d2f9ac06a.png) and $\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{v} = 34,$ then what is $\\dfrac{\\mathbf{u}\\cdot 2\\mathbf{v}}{3\\mathbf{v}\\cdot \\mathbf{v}}?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that the dot product is linear.\n\nTo quickly compute the numerator $\\mathbf{u} \\cdot 2\\mathbf{v},$ we compute $\\mathbf{u} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}$ first and then multiply the result by $2.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot 2v & =2(u\\cdot v) \\\\  & =2({u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4}) \\\\  & =2(7\\cdot 1+5\\cdot 2+(-9)\\cdot (-5)+3\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =2(7+10+45-6) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 56 \\\\  & =112.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo quickly compute the denominator $3\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{v},$ we multiply $\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{v}$ by $3.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3v\\cdot v & =3(v\\cdot v) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 34 \\\\  & =102.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathbf{u}\\cdot 2\\mathbf{v}}{3\\mathbf{v}\\cdot\\mathbf{v}} =\\dfrac{112}{102}=\\dfrac{56}{51}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271972 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133813 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98eb5973-f9b1-5c2f-8763-e8f8707e9b89" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Dot Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2949ad14-685b-5cfa-b653-00538bb33c4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269641} {:answer/id #uuid "29ed8c75-9864-53c6-a94a-55199c9f10b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269649} {:answer/id #uuid "2e61ce04-eaf7-59b6-a4b4-5058889ca29e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269686} {:answer/id #uuid "e0a6923d-4469-58c1-9685-e39f83dcf344" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271243} {:answer/id #uuid "e20610f9-2253-55bf-a043-ab145a04c7db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{34}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0a6923d-4469-58c1-9685-e39f83dcf344" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271243} :answer-field/id #uuid "e23ce2e0-7a68-5020-9922-0504b38bf059" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271907}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "49ee732b-c3e9-5be8-a3f6-6096638d8641" :question/math-academy-id "q-139948" :question/problem "If $u=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,,$ $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\,,$ and $u\\cdot v=-1,$ then $\\frac{(5u)\\cdot v}{v\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}v)}=$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that the dot product is linear.\n\nTo quickly compute the numerator $(5u)\\cdot v,$ we multiply $u\\cdot v$ by $5.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5u)\\cdot v & =5(u\\cdot v) \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo quickly compute the denominator $v\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}v),$ we compute $v\\cdot v$ first and then multiply the result by $\\frac{1}{2}.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}v) & =\\frac{1}{2}(v\\cdot v) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({v}_{1}^{2}+{v}_{2}^{2}+{v}_{3}^{2}+{v}_{4}^{2}+{v}_{5}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{5}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+4+4+0+25) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 34 \\\\  & =17.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{(5u)\\cdot v}{v\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}v)}=-\\frac{5}{17}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134201 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133813 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98eb5973-f9b1-5c2f-8763-e8f8707e9b89" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Dot Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "133eafdf-8b1b-5f1e-970a-ff8b46c6c703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324142} {:answer/id #uuid "4c334839-5b30-57fb-987f-491ba05009bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324595} {:answer/id #uuid "6f51e605-f913-5a44-9e67-faf1f088436f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324886} {:answer/id #uuid "e31ec296-1126-5c08-9cea-da8982c72d20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325967} {:answer/id #uuid "fe1135b3-8015-51a5-93d8-9fb4ab3c77bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe1135b3-8015-51a5-93d8-9fb4ab3c77bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326212} :answer-field/id #uuid "9db5292a-83c4-57af-ab9d-6fd9b12906d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326504}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "687f42ad-4d31-5616-a543-616339685f35" :question/math-academy-id "q-39205" :question/problem "If $u=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,,$ $v=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,,$ and $u\\cdot u=15,$ then $\\frac{u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}u)}{u\\cdot (\\frac{3}{4}v)}=$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that the dot product is linear.\n\nTo quickly compute the numerator $u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}u),$ we multiply $u\\cdot u$ by $\\frac{1}{2}.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}u) & =\\frac{1}{2}(u\\cdot u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 15 \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo quickly compute the denominator $u\\cdot (\\frac{3}{4}v),$ we compute $u\\cdot v$ first and then multiply the result by $\\frac{3}{4}.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot (\\frac{3}{4}v) & =\\frac{3}{4}(u\\cdot v) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}({u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}((-1)\\cdot (-3)+(-3)\\cdot (-1)+(-2)\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}(3+3+4+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{u\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}u)}{u\\cdot (\\frac{3}{4}v)} & =\\frac{(\\frac{15}{2})}{9}=\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342137 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133757 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fba8401-2da1-50f2-8e96-84cbe06d95fc" :knowledge-point/title "Applying Properties of the Dot Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47ab63dc-a690-5dd4-a5b1-0308bce544a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339764} {:answer/id #uuid "66df1f40-5fca-5c3c-8995-6aeb902c0315" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340033} {:answer/id #uuid "8ce0de76-0f94-5012-a0a0-1c54b5680920" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340393} {:answer/id #uuid "952ddb6f-eb5e-55f7-90bb-ac2200974f10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340475} {:answer/id #uuid "b398d76b-1b4f-5caf-93d3-bafb1e407fad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66df1f40-5fca-5c3c-8995-6aeb902c0315" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340033} :answer-field/id #uuid "49ee425f-fa02-5b6e-85ca-d79072d89494" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341507}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8427d1de-81ba-51ab-8f40-aacdc88c9eba" :question/math-academy-id "q-74041" :question/problem "Find $(3a-4b)\\cdot c+(2b)\\cdot d,$ if $c\\cdot a=-2,$ $c\\cdot b=4,$ and $d\\cdot b=5.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the fact that the dot product is linear in the first variable, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3a-4b)\\cdot c+(2b)\\cdot d & =(3a)\\cdot c-(4b)\\cdot c+(2b)\\cdot d \\\\  & =3(a\\cdot c)-4(b\\cdot c)+2(b\\cdot d).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we can use the fact that the dot product is symmetric to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(a\\cdot c)-4(b\\cdot c)+2(b\\cdot d) & =3(c\\cdot a)-4(c\\cdot b)+2(d\\cdot b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the values for $c\\cdot a,$ $c\\cdot b$ and $d\\cdot b,$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(c\\cdot a)-4(c\\cdot b)+2(d\\cdot b) & =3(-2)-4(4)+2(5) \\\\  & =-6-16+10 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(3a-4b)\\cdot c+(2d)\\cdot b=-12.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342140 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41c2d289-8df5-551b-9501-3a762c67386f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About the Dot Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cd32271-dc5a-5ef5-9de6-221af52fa72d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339519} {:answer/id #uuid "cb4de396-9b2c-5b13-aef6-a5e899e56c65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340929} {:answer/id #uuid "ed3be0c9-bb05-529a-b75a-d2226e0c878c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341221} {:answer/id #uuid "f00bbc30-d42f-57b1-a3ca-0b3e8b0038ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341243} {:answer/id #uuid "f2d2a831-2f45-5411-9d1a-f36b6ba2ec81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2cd32271-dc5a-5ef5-9de6-221af52fa72d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339519} :answer-field/id #uuid "60dfcf4a-5fb8-5dc8-acbc-e1191345ca9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341549}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "576bb373-56a3-5da0-ab43-2d02b000f274" :question/math-academy-id "q-74051" :question/problem "Suppose $(a+b)\\cdot a=0$, where $a\\ne 0.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $a\\cdot a=0$\n- $a\\cdot b>0$\n- $a\\cdot b<0$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $a\\ne 0$, we must have $a\\cdot a>0.$\n\n- Statement II is false while statement III is true. Simplifying the expression $(a+b)\\cdot a=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a+b)\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot a+b\\cdot a & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot a+a\\cdot b & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot b & =-a\\cdot a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $a\\ne 0$ implies that $a\\cdot a>0$, we obtain that $-a\\cdot a<0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"III only\"."}]}
